'''Wariguma''' ("moon fire") is a Guajicutean maize spirit that originated in the northern highlands during the 1800s. It began as a rough home-distilled moonshine made by Guaji households and small farms, using maize that was available in the interior and north. Over time it became a familiar local drink, and in the modern era it was formalized into a regulated domestic spirit. It remains less popular internationally than [[Bariña]], but its status has risen inside Guajicutea.

== Description ==

Wariguma is usually clear or only lightly tinted, with a pale straw appearance when fresh. It is stronger and drier than Bariña, with a grain-forward profile and a sharper finish. Common tasting notes include toasted maize, raw grain, light smoke, pepper, and a faint earthy sweetness. Small-farm batches can be rougher and more aromatic, while commercial bottles are cleaner and more uniform.

It is usually served neat, though some drinkers chill it before serving. In the north, it is often associated with winter gatherings, field work, and household production rather than export drinking.

== History ==

=== Northern origins ===

Wariguma emerged in the northern Guaji interior during the 1800s, when maize had become a common crop in the region. Households and small farms distilled surplus grain into a strong spirit that could be stored, shared, or sold locally. The earliest Wariguma was not a prestige drink. It was a practical rural product made with simple equipment and local knowledge.

Its early reputation was tied to the conditions of production. It was made in small batches, often in private stills, and was associated with households rather than formal industry. For much of its history, it remained a local northern drink with little presence outside Guajicutea.

=== Formalization ===

In the modern period, the Guajicutean state brought Wariguma into a formal regulatory framework. Licensed producers began to bottle and distribute it more widely, and quality standards made it easier to sell in urban markets. This process did not erase the older farm-made style, but it gave Wariguma a more stable commercial identity.

[[File:Wariguma farm still.png|thumb|right|A small northern still used for farm-made Wariguma]]

As formalization continued, Wariguma moved from a rough regional moonshine toward a recognized domestic spirit. It became more visible in restaurants, specialty shops, and heritage markets, though it still lacked the international reach of Bariña.

== Cultural significance ==

Wariguma is closely associated with northern Guaji identity and with maize agriculture. It is often treated as a more local and more rustic drink than Bariña, but that same reputation has helped its domestic standing grow. Among many drinkers, it now signals regional character, rural continuity, and a more serious claim to Guajicutean tradition.

The drink also reflects the broader shift in Guajicutean food culture from improvised household production to regulated heritage products. Wariguma remains rooted in the northern small-farm tradition, but it is no longer treated as merely improvised moonshine.

